# Requirements Package Standard

## Purpose

Use this rule when creating or updating a requirements package under
`docs/requirements/<module-or-topic>/`.

## Required Core Files

Every module-level documentation package must contain:

```text
docs/requirements/<name>/
  README.md
  prd.md
  specification.md
```

Optional files are added only when the topic needs them:

```text
brainstorm.md
policies.md
agent-protocol.md
ci-protocol.md
artifact-lifecycle.md
metrics-and-validation.md
implementation-plan.md
schemas/*.json
```

## Required Metadata

Every Markdown document must include:

```markdown
Version: x.y.z
```

The field must be directly under the H1. Increment the version whenever the
document changes.

## README Contract

`README.md` must state:

- package purpose;
- current status;
- document index;
- scope and non-goals;
- related modules.

## PRD Contract

`prd.md` must state:

- problem;
- goal;
- users;
- requirements;
- success criteria, split into two lists (see Success Contract below);
- risks;
- recommendation.

### Success Contract

The PRD's success criteria are two lists, never one:

- **Release criteria** are checkable when the work ships: a test, a command, a
  file, a count. Each is something a release can be blocked on.
- **Outcome criteria** are what should be true some time after release: adoption,
  a measured change, a behavior seen in use. Each outcome criterion either names
  the observation that would confirm it (what is looked at, where, and when) or
  declares `not measured — <reason>`.

An outcome criterion with neither an observation nor a `not measured — <reason>`
is not a criterion. A list whose every entry is `not measured` is acceptable: it
is honest. A PRD with no outcome list at all is not.

## Specification Contract

`specification.md` must state:

- module identity;
- storage structure;
- manifest/config shape;
- CLI or skill surface;
- data contracts;
- integration points;
- acceptance criteria;
- a verification field on every requirement (see Verification Field below).

### Verification Field

Every requirement in the specification carries one verification field, in the
same vocabulary a flow criterion's trailing marker uses:

```markdown
Verification: exec `<command>`            # a command whose exit code decides it
Verification: invariant `<command>`       # a command that must keep passing
Verification: judged                      # a person reads the evidence
Verification: none — <reason>             # honestly unverifiable, with the reason
```

`exec` and `invariant` name a backticked command; `none` names its reason after
an em dash. A requirement with no field is a defect, not a `none`.

## Verification Contract

Before finishing:

- verify required files exist;
- verify all Markdown files have `Version`;
- verify README links to all package files;
- verify schemas are referenced from specification when present;
- verify status is honest: `draft`, `spec ready`, `implemented`, or `future`;
- verify every specification requirement carries a `Verification:` field, and
  fail the package when one does not, naming that requirement;
- verify the PRD has both a release-criteria list and an outcome-criteria list,
  and fail the package when the outcome list is missing (an outcome list whose
  entries are all `not measured — <reason>` passes);
- update `docs/requirements/roadmap.md` when the package represents a module or
  standard capability.

## Review Contract

Documentation review must check:

- completeness against this rule;
- contradictions across README/PRD/spec;
- unsupported implementation claims;
- missing integration points;
- stale roadmap or version references;
- unsafe overpromising versus current implementation.
